WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.900
Welcome to Grade 8 English, Unit
9: Music as Universal Language.

2
00:00:05.260 --> 00:00:11.680
This unit is called "Music as Universal Language"
because you will explore how music, performance,

3
00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:17.020
and the creative arts bring people together
across cultures, languages, and backgrounds.

4
00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:21.980
Music is one of the most powerful forms of human expression.

5
00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:26.340
It can communicate emotions that words alone cannot capture.

6
00:00:27.110 --> 00:00:30.080
A song can make you feel joy, sorrow, excitement,

7
00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:35.260
or peace without you understanding a single
word of the language in which it is sung.

8
00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:40.720
That is why we call music a universal
language: it speaks to everyone.

9
00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:46.640
In this short lecture, preview the key
ideas and prepare for the independent lesson.

10
00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:48.900
By the end of this unit,

11
00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:55.780
you will be able to explain what it means to call music a
universal language and discuss how the performing arts connect

12
00:00:55.780 --> 00:00:57.060
people across cultures.

13
00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:04.640
You will also read the story "The Curtain Never Lies" and
answer comprehension and analysis questions using evidence

14
00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:05.540
from the text.

15
00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:12.260
You will also use thirty key vocabulary words
related to performance, creativity, emotion,

16
00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:15.220
and integrity in your own speaking and writing.

17
00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:18.500
Keep these goals in mind as you work.

18
00:01:19.180 --> 00:01:27.280
Your vocabulary work begins with these words: operational,
rationalisation, interdependence, prose-rhythm, verse-form,

19
00:01:27.850 --> 00:01:28.250
free-verse.

20
00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:33.720
Listen to each pronunciation in the
vocabulary lab, study its meaning in context,

21
00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:37.460
and use the word accurately in
your own discussion and writing.

22
00:01:38.230 --> 00:01:42.240
The reading sequence includes What
Makes Music a Universal Language?

23
00:01:42.890 --> 00:01:47.040
and Behind the Curtain: How a
School Production Comes Together.

24
00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:49.000
Read actively.

25
00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:55.020
Identify each text's central ideas,
notice viewpoint and reliability,

26
00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:59.300
and support every inference with
relevant evidence from the text.

27
00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:03.620
The first language focus is Present Perfect Continuous.

28
00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:10.440
The Present Perfect Continuous describes actions
that began in the past and are still running now,

29
00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:14.180
or that have only just stopped and left visible evidence,

30
00:02:14.180 --> 00:02:17.880
as when paint-stained hands tell
you someone has been painting.

31
00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:25.580
It is built with has or have, then
been, then the -ing form of the verb,

32
00:02:26.140 --> 00:02:31.900
and it puts the spotlight on how long an activity
has lasted rather than on whether it finished.

33
00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:39.080
Notice the structure in the model examples, then apply
it deliberately in connected sentences and paragraphs.

34
00:02:39.880 --> 00:02:45.560
In speaking, you will complete Speaking
1: Role-Play Interview with a Performer.

35
00:02:45.930 --> 00:02:50.440
Organise your ideas, use evidence
and respond clearly to questions.

36
00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:54.700
In writing, you will plan, draft, check and revise.

37
00:02:55.540 --> 00:02:58.740
Use the success criteria before submitting your work.

38
00:02:59.260 --> 00:03:00.780
You are ready to begin.

39
00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:07.380
Start with the vocabulary lab, continue through
reading and comprehension, apply the grammar focus,

40
00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:10.180
and complete the speaking and writing practices.

41
00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:18.600
Use the AI tutor for hints and feedback any time, and
bring your questions to a live session too if you join one.
